---
import Highlight from '../components/Highlight.astro';
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import { LEGAL, controllerLine, updatedLong } from '../data/legal';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Privacy · The Lazy Horseman"
  description="What The Lazy Horseman collects, why, who else can see it, and how to get it back or get rid of it."
>
  <section class="band cream-band page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The boring but important bit</p>
      <h1>What we do with your <Highlight>details</Highlight></h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Written to be read, not to be survived. If anything here is unclear, ask
        and I will fix the wording.
      </p>
      <p class="legal-date">Last updated {updatedLong}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band cream-band">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="prose legal">
        <h2>The short version</h2>
        <p>
          The newsletter needs your email address and nothing else. The booking
          tool needs an email address, a password and your name, so a yard knows
          who has the arena at six. Nothing is sold, nothing is shared for
          advertising, and there are no tracking cookies on this site at all.
        </p>

        <h2>Who is responsible</h2>
        <p>
          {controllerLine} is the data controller for this website, the
          newsletter and accounts on the booking tool. Everything below is my
          doing, and{' '}
          <a href={`mailto:${LEGAL.email}`}>{LEGAL.email}</a> reaches me.
        </p>
        {LEGAL.address ? (
          <p class="legal-address">
            {LEGAL.address.map((line) => <span>{line}</span>)}
          </p>
        ) : (
          <p>A postal address is available on request, by email.</p>
        )}
        {LEGAL.icoNumber ? (
          <p>
            Registered with the Information Commissioner's Office, number{' '}
            {LEGAL.icoNumber}.
          </p>
        ) : null}

        <h2>What is collected, and why</h2>

        <h3>If you read the newsletter</h3>
        <p>
          Your email address, the date you subscribed and the exact wording you
          agreed to. That last one exists so that if you ever ask why you are
          getting this, there is an honest answer rather than a shrug. The
          lawful basis is your consent, and every issue has an unsubscribe link
          that works immediately.
        </p>

        <h3>If you run a yard on the booking tool</h3>
        <p>
          Your name, email address and a password, which is stored hashed and is
          not readable by anybody including me. Your yard's name, its address on
          this domain, its timezone, its facilities and its opening hours. When
          paid plans start, a customer reference from the payment provider, but
          never your card details, which go straight to them and never touch
          this service. The lawful basis is performing the contract you have
          with me.
        </p>

        <h3>If you book at a yard</h3>
        <p>
          Your name, email address and password, your horses' names, and the
          slots you book, including which horse each one was for. Your yard can
          see all of that. Other riders cannot: the booking grid shows them that
          a slot is taken, never by whom.
        </p>
        <p>
          If your yard invites you by email, they type your address in before
          you have any account here, and it sits in their invite list until you
          accept or it runs out after thirty days. They are responsible for
          having a reason to contact you. See <a href="#yards">yards and their
          own responsibilities</a> below.
        </p>

        <h3>Whoever you are</h3>
        <p>
          Signing in records the time, your IP address and your browser's user
          agent. That is how anybody spots a compromised account, and it is kept
          because not keeping it would be worse.
        </p>

        <h2>Who else it touches</h2>
        <p>
          Running any of this means other companies process some of it on my
          instructions. These are the only ones.
        </p>
        {/* TODO when the newsletter is wired to Buttondown: add a row for
            them, "The newsletter list", and check where they host it. The
            signup forms on this site do nothing at the moment, so naming
            a processor that is not yet processing would be worse than
            leaving them out. */}
        <div class="legal-table-wrap">
          <table class="legal-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Who</th><th>What for</th><th>Where</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Supabase</td>
                <td>The database, and sign in</td>
                <td>London</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Resend</td>
                <td>Sending email, including invites and confirmations</td>
                <td>Ireland</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Vercel</td>
                <td>Hosting and serving the pages</td>
                <td>UK and elsewhere</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Hostinger</td>
                <td>The domain, and the mailbox you reach me on</td>
                <td>EU</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Vercel serves requests from wherever is nearest, which can be outside
          the UK. That transfer is covered by their standard contractual clauses
          and the UK addendum to them.
        </p>

        <h2 id="yards">Yards, and what they are responsible for</h2>
        <p>
          A yard decides who is on its list, who gets invited and who is taken
          off. For that list and for the bookings on it, the yard is the
          controller and I am only the processor: I hold it and show it to the
          people the yard has allowed, and I do nothing else with it.
        </p>
        <p>
          So if you want to be taken off a yard's list, ask the yard. If you
          want your whole account gone, ask me and it goes, from every yard at
          once.
        </p>

        <h2>How long it is kept</h2>
        <p>
          Your account and its bookings stay while the account exists. Ask for
          it to be deleted and it goes within thirty days, along with your
          horses and your place on every yard.
        </p>
        <p>
          A booking you cancel is marked cancelled rather than erased, so the
          yard's own diary still adds up. Cancelled bookings go when the account
          does. Newsletter unsubscribes keep the email address on a suppression
          list, because the alternative is emailing you again by accident.
        </p>

        <h2>Cookies</h2>
        <p>
          This site sets none. No analytics, no advertising, no pixels, nothing
          that follows you anywhere, which is why there is no cookie banner
          getting in your way.
        </p>
        <p>
          The booking tool sets one cookie, which is what keeps you signed in.
          It is strictly necessary and there is no version of signing in that
          works without it.
        </p>

        <h2>Children</h2>
        <p>
          Plenty of riders are under sixteen. Accounts are meant for people aged
          thirteen and over. If a younger rider needs to book, the yard should
          book on their behalf rather than setting them up with an account. If
          you believe a child under thirteen has an account, tell me and I will
          remove it.
        </p>

        <h2>What you can ask for</h2>
        <p>
          You can ask for a copy of everything held about you, ask for it to be
          corrected, ask for it to be deleted, ask me to stop using it, or
          object to how it is being used. You can withdraw consent to the
          newsletter at any time, with one click, at the bottom of every issue.
        </p>
        <p>
          Email <a href={`mailto:${LEGAL.email}`}>{LEGAL.email}</a> and you will
          get an answer within a month, usually within a couple of days, because
          this is not a big company.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you are not happy with the answer you can complain to the
          Information Commissioner's Office at{' '}
          <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/" rel="noopener">ico.org.uk</a>,
          or on 0303 123 1113. I would rather you told me first so I can put it
          right.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes</h2>
        <p>
          If this changes in a way that matters, the date at the top changes and
          anyone with an account gets an email about it. Small corrections to
          wording just get made.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
